<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Exploring Related Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00154</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Exploring Related Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>93418012-ce14-4f37-9d9c-55a2b8ef6b59</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">With a partner, discuss the following situations and equations. For
        each, consider:</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>What does the solution mean in the context of the situation?</item>
        <item>Are the given values solutions?</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00002">If operations are applied correctly, the solution to an equation is also
        the solution to all equations equivalent to it. If operations are incorrectly applied, the
        solution of each equation is different.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">For numbers 1 and 2, use the following situation and equation:</para>
      <para id="para-00004">Noah is buying a pair of jeans and using a coupon for 10% off. The total
        price is $56.70, which includes $2.70 in sales tax. Noah’s purchase can be modeled by the
        equation:</para>
      <para id="para-00005"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para><!--Q1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">What does the solution to the equation mean in this situation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00008">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The solution is the
            price of a pair of jeans.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">How can you verify that 70 is not a solution but 60 is the solution?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00011">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <para id="para-00012">70 can be substituted into the equation to make a false statement: <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>70</m:mn>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>70</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>65.70</m:mn>
                <m:mi>≠</m:mi>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> ❌</para>
          <para id="para-00013">60 can be substituted into the equation to make a true statement: <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>60</m:mn>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>60</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>✓</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00014">Here are some equations that are related to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Each equation is a
            result of performing one or more moves on that original equation. Each can also be
            interpreted in terms of Noah’s purchase.</para>
          <para id="para-00015">For numbers 3 – 5, use the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>100</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>270</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>670</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00016">What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00018">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Every term was
            multiplied by 100. The equation is equivalent to Noah’s equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00019">What is the interpretation of this new equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00021">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The price is now
            expressed in cents instead of dollars.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">Is the solution the same?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00024">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 60 is a solution in both
            equations.</para>
          <para id="para-00025">For numbers 6 – 8, use the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>54</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00026">What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00028">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Subtract 2.70 from both
            sides of the equation. The equation is equivalent to Noah’s equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">What is the interpretation of this new equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00031">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The price is still in
            dollars.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00032">Is the solution the same?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00034">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 60 is a solution in both
            equations.</para>
          <para id="para-00035">For numbers 9 – 11, use the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0.9</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00036">What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00037">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00038">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The terms <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and 0.1x were correctly
            combined to create an equivalent equation to Noah’s original equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00039">What is the interpretation of this new equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00041">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00042">10% off means paying 90% of the original price. 90% of the original
            price plus sales tax is $56.70.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q11-->
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00043">Is the solution the same?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">
          <para id="para-00044">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00045">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00046">60 is a solution in both equations.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00047">For numbers 12 – 14, use the equation <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <!--Q12-->
      <exercise id="exer-00012">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00048">What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">
          <para id="para-00049">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00050">Sales tax was not included on the left but was included on the
            right. This equation is not equivalent to Noah’s original equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q13-->
      <exercise id="exer-00013">
        <problem id="prob-00013">
          <para id="para-00051">What is the interpretation of this new equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00013">
          <para id="para-00052">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00053">The price after using the coupon for 10% off before sales tax is not
            the same as the price after using the coupon for 10% off including the sales tax,
            $56.70.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q14-->
      <exercise id="exer-00014">
        <problem id="prob-00014">
          <para id="para-00054">Is the solution the same?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00014">
          <para id="para-00055">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00056">60 is not a solution to this equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00057">For numbers 15–17, use the equation <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>59.40</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <!--Q15-->
      <exercise id="exer-00015">
        <problem id="prob-00015">
          <para id="para-00058">What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00015">
          <para id="para-00059">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Subtract 2.70 from the
            left and add 2.70 to the right.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q16-->
      <exercise id="exer-00016">
        <problem id="prob-00016">
          <para id="para-00060">What is the interpretation of this new equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00016">
          <para id="para-00061">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00062">The same operation must be done to both sides of an equation to keep
            it balanced and to create an equivalent equation. The price after using the coupon for
            10% off is not equal to 56.70 plus tax.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q17-->
      <exercise id="exer-00017">
        <problem id="prob-00017">
          <para id="para-00063">Is the solution the same?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00017">
          <para id="para-00064">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 60 is not a solution to
            this equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00065">For numbers 18–20, use the equation <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <!--Q18-->
      <exercise id="exer-00018">
        <problem id="prob-00018">
          <para id="para-00066">What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00018">
          <para id="para-00067">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The left side only was
            multiplied by 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q19-->
      <exercise id="exer-00019">
        <problem id="prob-00019">
          <para id="para-00068">What is the interpretation of this new equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00019">
          <para id="para-00069">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Two pairs of jeans after
            the 10% discount and sales tax cost 56.70.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q20-->
      <exercise id="exer-00020">
        <problem id="prob-00020">
          <para id="para-00070">Is the solution the same?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00020">
          <para id="para-00071">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 60 is not a solution to
            this equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="_9f003b7b-c462-41fb-9132-5dfd019c4547" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00021">
          <para id="para-00072">Based on your work above, which of the six equations are equivalent
            to the original equation, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>? Select the <emphasis>
            three</emphasis> equations that are equivalent to the original.</para>
          <list number-style="lower-alpha" list-type="enumerated"  id="list-00002">
            <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
                <mn>100</mn>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>10</mn>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>270</mn>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mn>5670</mn>
              </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>0.1</mn>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mn>54</mn>
              </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
                <mn>0.9</mn>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2.70</mn>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mn>56.70</mn>
              </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>0.1</mn>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mn>56.70</mn>
              </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>0.1</mn>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mn>59.40</mn>
              </math></item>
            <item xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>−<!-- − --></mo>
                <mn>0.1</mn>
                <mi>x</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2.70</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>=</mo>
                <mn>56.70</mn>
              </math></item>
          </list>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00021" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
          <para id="para-00073">Compare your answer: A, B, C were all equivalent equations to the
            original with 60 as a solution.</para>
          <para id="para-00074"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>100</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>270</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5670</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0.1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>54</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, and <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0.9</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2.70</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>56.70</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Looking at Equivalent Equations</title>
      <para id="para-00075">Watch the following video to learn more about why these are equivalent
        equations.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://openstax.org/r/equivalent-equations" />
      </media>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00022">
          <problem id="prob-00024">
            <para id="para-00076"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_1_6_3_SC" /></para>
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Properties of Equality</title>
        <note id="note-00002">
          <title>Properties of Equality</title>
    <para id="para-00077">When you add, subtract,
          multiply, or divide the same quantity from both sides of an equation, you still have
          equality.</para>
    <table class="unnumbered os-raise-textheavytable unnumbered"
            id="table-00001">
            <tgroup cols="2">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry align="left">
                    <para id="para-00078">Subtraction Property of Equality</para>
                  <para
                      id="para-00079">For any real numbers <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          , <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>,
          and <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,if <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,then <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                          <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                          <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          .</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry align="left">
                    <para id="para-00080">Addition Property of Equality</para>
                  <para id="para-00081">For
          any real numbers <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          , <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>,
          and <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,if <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,then <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          .</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry align="left">
                    <para id="para-00082">Division Property of Equality</para>
                  <para id="para-00083">For
          any real numbers <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          , <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>,
          and <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>,
          and <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>≠</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,if <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,then <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mfrac>
                            <m:mrow>
                              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                            </m:mrow>
                            <m:mrow>
                              <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                            </m:mrow>
                          </m:mfrac>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mfrac>
                            <m:mrow>
                              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                            </m:mrow>
                            <m:mrow>
                              <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                            </m:mrow>
                          </m:mfrac>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          .</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry align="left">
                    <para id="para-00084">Multiplication Property of Equality</para>
                  <para
                      id="para-00085">For any real numbers <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          , <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>,
          and <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,if <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          ,then <m:math display="inline">
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                          <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
          .</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </note>
        <para id="para-00086">For equations to be equivalent, inverse operations are used and must
          be applied to both sides of an equation so it remains balanced.</para>
        <para id="para-00087">Inverse operations are operations that “undo” other operations.</para>
        <section id="sect-00006">
          <para id="para-00107"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
          <para id="para-00088">Solve <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>15</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00089">Since 3 is being multiplied by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, to solve for <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, divide both sides by 3.</para>
          <para id="para-00090"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>15</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00091"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </section>
        <section id="sect-00007">
          <para id="para-00108"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
          <para id="para-00092">Solve <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>12</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00093"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Subtract 4 from both sides.<newline /> <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00094"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Simplify.<newline /> <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00095"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Multiply both sides by 2.<newline /> <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>×</m:mi>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mi>×</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00096"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.<newline /> <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </section>
        <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00008">
          <note class="try" id="note-00003">
            <title>Try It: Properties of Equality</title>   
        <para id="para-00097">For questions 1 - 2,
            use the scenario:</para>
        <para id="para-00098">Denae bought 6 pounds of grapes for
            $10.74.</para>
            <!--Q1-->
        <exercise id="exer-00021">
              <problem id="prob-00022">
                <para id="para-00099">Write an equation for the situation.</para>
              </problem>
              <solution id="sol-00022">
                <para id="para-00100">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a
            sample.</para>
                <para id="para-00101">Let <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>=one
            pound of grapes.</para>
                <para id="para-00102">6g=10.74</para>
              </solution>
            </exercise>
            <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <exercise
              id="_743bc613-9ed4-4785-994f-65c52046a9b4" class="multiple-choice">
              <problem id="prob-00023">
                <para id="para-00103">Solve the equation.</para>
              </problem>
              <solution id="sol-00023">
                <para id="para-00104">1.79</para>
                <para id="para-00111"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the variable.</para>
                <para><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>: cost
            for one pound of grapes</para>
                <para id="para-00106"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Write an equation.</para>
                <para><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                      <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>10.74</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math></para>
                <para id="para-00109"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for g.</para>
                <para>Divide BOTH sides by 6. Divide BOTH sides by 6.</para>
                <para><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mfrac>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                          <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                        </m:mrow>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:mfrac>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mfrac>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mn>10.74</m:mn>
                        </m:mrow>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:mfrac>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math></para>
                <para id="para-00110"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify to solve.</para>
                <para>Simplify to solve.</para>
                <para><m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                      <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>1.79</m:mn>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math></para>
                <para id="para-00105">The grapes cost $1.79 per pound.</para>

              </solution>
            </exercise>
          </note>
        </section>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>